    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1127">

                <pb facs="1044" n="786" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg786"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/2164</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spring
                        Rice</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                    
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 7, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received May 10</hi>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Lansing</hi>: In connection with the
                    arguments which are now being advanced in various quarters with regard to the
                    propriety of the exportation of arms from the United States for the use of the
                    Allies, I have obtained some details, which may be of interest, as to the
                    exports of munitions of war from Germany to Great Britain during the Boer
                    war.</p>
                <p>It appears that, after the Boer republics had been cut off from communication
                    with Europe by sea, 108 15-pounder quick-firing guns and 500 rounds of
                    ammunition for each were bought from the firm of Erhardt for the use of the
                    British Army. Besides this order <pb facs="1045" n="787" xml:id="pg787"/> large
                    numbers of guns, rifles, and swords, and also large quantities of shell,
                    gunpowder, and small arms ammunition were imported into England from Germany
                    during the war.</p>
                <p>Believe me [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cecil Spring Rice</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
